//! core/host/testing — the test runner's platform, one double per effect.
//!
//! Only the module that exports `main` may import `core/host`. This is the same
//! surface for a test source, and the `testing` segment in the path is the whole
//! of that rule (SPEC 4.1.1).
//!
//! Every name here is `core/host`'s name — `alloc`, `stdout`, `stderr`,
//! `stdin`, `fs`, `net`, `clock`, `rand`, `entropy`, `env`, `proc`, `sockets` —
//! but **called** rather than referred to. `core/host`'s `clock` is one clock
//! because a process has one. `clock()` is a fresh one every call, so what one
//! test does to it is invisible to the next.
//!
//! Configuration is a **method on the value that answers a new handle**:
//! `clock().at(1000)`, `rand().seed(7)`,
//! `env().variables([...]).arguments([...])`.
//! There is no free-function spelling of any of them.
//!
//! Most structs carry an `I64` handle rather than their state. A captured stdout
//! accumulates what a test writes to it, and Buri has no mutation, so the state
//! lives on the runner's side and the value here names it.
//!
//! `TestNet` is the exception. Its configuration is a **responder**, which is
//! behaviour rather than state, and behaviour is what a runner written in Rust
//! and JavaScript cannot hold for a program. So it carries the function itself
//! and `fetch` is a Buri body. The handle beside the responder names its **log**,
//! and a log is state like any other.
//!
//! `TestFs` is the second value that is not a bare handle. A fault plan holds
//! `IoError` *values* and `IoError.Other` carries a `Str`, which
//! `cli/runtime/lib.rs` §2.1 cannot hand back across a row. So the plan travels
//! in the program, and the twelve methods of `FsRead` and `FsWrite` are Buri
//! bodies over rows that take the handle. The runner holds the log, the files,
//! and which entries of the plan have fired.
//!
//! **One `TestFs` implements both effects, and a context that reads and writes
//! has to bind that one value to both names.** Two `fs()` calls are two
//! filesystems, so `FsRead: fs(), FsWrite: fs()` is a world where a write lands
//! where the read cannot see it. A block that does both therefore names its
//! double first:
//!
//! ```buri ignore why="a test source is the one place a `test` block is legal, and this file is not one"
//! let disk = fs().files([("config.toml", "port=8080")]);
//! let ctx = context { Alloc: alloc(), FsRead: disk, FsWrite: disk };
//! ```
//!
//! A *named* `context` declaration cannot do that at all — its bindings are two
//! separate expressions — so a declaration binds the half it needs, which is
//! usually `FsRead`.
//!
//! ## The call log
//!
//! `TestFs`, `TestNet` and `TestStdin` each keep every call made through the
//! handle, and `calls()` answers them **in the order they completed**. The log is
//! for the assertions an outcome cannot make: that a cache was consulted once and
//! not twice, that nothing was written at all, that a retry went to the same URL.
//! One `Call` record per double, built by a constructor named after the method it
//! records, compared by `Eq` and printed by `Show`.
//!
//! `TestProc` has nothing to read back. `exitWith` absorbs the call so that a
//! test which exits keeps running, and what a test asserts is what it does
//! *after* the exit.
//!
//! ## The fault plan
//!
//! `fs().faults([...])` and `net().faults([...])` say what breaks. A fault is one
//! of those same `Call` constructors and an error: `readFile(p).fails(e)` for
//! every matching call, `readFile(p).failsOnCall(n, e)` for the `n`th. Matching
//! is the `Eq` the `Call` records already derive, so a fault is spelled exactly
//! as `calls()` reports the call it names. **Success comes from the
//! environment and failure comes from the plan**: `files` and `respond` say what
//! a call finds, and nothing else in the module says what a call answers.
//!
//! **A fault whose call never happens fails the test.** The runner checks it
//! after every `test` body and reports the plans with entries nothing fired.
//!
//! ## A socket with no network behind it
//!
//! `sockets()` is the one double a program takes a **handle out of**: `open()`
//! mints a `Socket` and `sent()` reads back `[(Socket, Message)]`. A socket is
//! the one thing in `core/net/server` a test cannot write down for itself,
//! because a `Socket` a program invents names nothing.
//!
//! It is only the writing half. Reading a socket belongs to `Listen`, and a fake
//! acceptor is a value a test writes for itself. What this closes is the other
//! half: a hand-written `Sockets` records nothing, because an effect method takes
//! only `self` and `self` is immutable.
//!
//! ## The order the work happens in
//!
//! `tasks()` is the one double whose subject is not state but **scheduling**.
//! `Tasks.parallel` promises its results in the items' order and promises nothing
//! about the order the work runs in, so a program that depended on the second
//! would pass every test and fail in production. This double makes the order a
//! value a test writes down: program order by default, `anyOrder()` for one
//! seeded order, and `everyOrder()` for all of them. `everyOrder()` re-runs the
//! `test` body once per completion order, so the assertion at the end of it is an
//! assertion about every order. A seed is the order's own number, so a failure
//! names a line that replays it.
//!
//! **It covers `scope` and `spawn` too.** A scope collects what was spawned and
//! runs a round of it through `Tasks.parallel`, so the order the double chooses
//! for a `parallel` is the order the spawned tasks run in. Nothing here waits on
//! real time either: a task that sleeps sleeps on `clock()`, which moves a
//! counter.
//!
//! ## `arguments`
//!
//! A type's methods are one map keyed by name, so `Env`'s reader and this
//! builder cannot both be `arguments`. The reader is `args(self): [Str]` on
//! `core/effect`'s `Env` — the name `core/env` already used — which leaves
//! `TestEnv.arguments([Str])` as the builder.

from "core/effect" import {
    Alloc, Clock, Connected, Entropy, Env, Frame, Header, IoError, Method, Net,
    NetError, Proc, Rand, Received, Region, Request, Response, ServeError, Sockets,
    Stderr, Stdin, Stdout, Tasks, WebSocketClient,
};

// The filesystem's two effects, declared in `core/fs` because their methods
// name a `Path`.
from "core/fs" import { FsRead, FsWrite };

// The one module above `core/effect` this one imports, and it is the surface
// rather than the authority: `Socket` and `Message` are inert data that
// `core/net/server` declares.
from "core/net/server" import { Message, Socket };
from "core/path" import { Path };

// --- Alloc --------------------------------------------------------------------

/// Real, from a per-test arena the runner reclaims.
///
/// The one double with no state at all. The charge comes from the *types*
/// (`core/effect`'s `Alloc`), so `allocate` answers the byte count it was asked
/// for and the handle names nothing.
export struct TestAlloc(I64);

impl Alloc for TestAlloc {
    fn allocate(self, bytes: Int): Region;
}

export fn alloc(): TestAlloc;

// --- Output -------------------------------------------------------------------

/// Captured, and never printed. `captured` is the only way to read it back, so a
/// test's output is something to assert on rather than something to watch scroll
/// past.
export struct TestStdout(I64);

impl Stdout for TestStdout {
    /// Always `.Ok(())`: a captured stream is a buffer the runner owns, so there
    /// is nothing here to fail.
    fn print(self, text: Template): Result<(), IoError>;

    fn println(self, text: Template): Result<(), IoError>;

    /// Captured as the UTF-8 text the octets spell, so `captured` answers one
    /// question rather than two.
    fn writeBytes(self, b: [U8]): Result<(), IoError>;
}

export fn stdout(): TestStdout;

impl TestStdout {
    /// Everything written to this stream so far.
    export fn captured(self): Str;
}

/// The same, for standard error.
export struct TestStderr(I64);

impl Stderr for TestStderr {
    fn eprint(self, text: Template): Result<(), IoError>;

    fn eprintln(self, text: Template): Result<(), IoError>;
}

export fn stderr(): TestStderr;

impl TestStderr {
    /// Everything written to this stream so far.
    export fn captured(self): Str;
}

// --- Input --------------------------------------------------------------------

/// End of input, until a test says otherwise: `readLine` and `readBytes` both
/// answer `.None`.
///
/// `core/host`'s `stdin` is the process's, which a test must never read: a suite
/// that blocked on a pipe nobody was writing to would hang rather than fail.
export struct TestStdin(I64);

impl Stdin for TestStdin {
    fn readLine(self): Option<Str>;

    fn readBytes(self, n: Int): Option<[U8]>;
}

export fn stdin(): TestStdin;

impl TestStdin {
    /// A **new** stdin reading those lines, then end of input.
    export fn lines(self, lines: [Str]): TestStdin;

    /// A **new** stdin reading those octets, then end of input.
    ///
    /// `lines` and `bytes` **replace** each other rather than composing. A stream
    /// is either the lines a test wrote or the octets it wrote, so a stdin built
    /// from octets answers `.None` to `readLine`, and the last builder in the
    /// chain is the stream.
    export fn bytes(self, b: [U8]): TestStdin;

    /// Every read made through this handle, in the order they completed.
    ///
    /// A read that ran off the end of the stream is a call like any other: the
    /// log records what the code under test asked for, not what it was given.
    export fn calls(self): [StdinCall];
}

// --- Filesystem ---------------------------------------------------------------

/// In-memory and empty. Writes are visible to that test and discarded after it.
///
/// A file holds **octets**, and `readFile` decodes them as UTF-8 the way a real
/// filesystem's does — so `writeFileBytes` of a byte a text encoding cannot carry
/// reads back through `readFileBytes` unchanged.
///
/// An in-memory filesystem is thinner than a real one in two observable places.
/// It is a flat map of paths, so the parents of a fixture's path are not
/// directories until `makeDir` says so; and `sync` has nothing to flush, so it is
/// `.Ok` for any path that exists. Everything else — append creating a file,
/// rename replacing one, removing what is not there — answers exactly what a real
/// filesystem answers.
///
/// ## Two fields, for `TestNet`'s reason
///
/// `handle` names the runner-side state — the files, the attenuation, the log,
/// and the plan's own bookkeeping — and `plan` is the fault plan, which stays in
/// the program because `cli/runtime/lib.rs` §2.1 cannot hand an error variant
/// with a field back across a row. So the match is the `Eq` the `FsCall` records
/// derive, and every method here asks the plan before it calls its row.
export struct TestFs {
    handle: I64,
    plan: [FsFault],
}

impl FsRead for TestFs {
    fn readFile(self, path: Path): Result<Str, IoError> {
        let at = path.text();
        match (self.planned(readFile(at))) {
            .Some(error) => .Err(error),
            .None => fsReadFile(self.handle, at),
        }
    }

    /// The one method of the filesystem that cannot fail, so no fault ever fires
    /// on it. A plan naming it is a plan whose call never happens, which fails the
    /// test at the end of the block.
    fn fileExists(self, path: Path): Bool {
        fsFileExists(self.handle, path.text())
    }

    fn readDir(self, path: Path): Result<[Str], IoError> {
        let at = path.text();
        match (self.planned(readDir(at))) {
            .Some(error) => .Err(error),
            .None => fsReadDir(self.handle, at),
        }
    }

    fn readFileBytes(self, path: Path): Result<[U8], IoError> {
        let at = path.text();
        match (self.planned(readFileBytes(at))) {
            .Some(error) => .Err(error),
            .None => fsReadFileBytes(self.handle, at),
        }
    }
}

impl FsWrite for TestFs {
    fn writeFile(self, path: Path, body: Str): Result<(), IoError> {
        let at = path.text();
        match (self.planned(writeFile(at, body))) {
            .Some(error) => .Err(error),
            .None => fsWriteFile(self.handle, at, body),
        }
    }

    fn writeFileBytes(self, path: Path, body: [U8]): Result<(), IoError> {
        let at = path.text();
        match (self.planned(writeFileBytes(at, body))) {
            .Some(error) => .Err(error),
            .None => fsWriteFileBytes(self.handle, at, body),
        }
    }

    fn appendFile(self, path: Path, body: [U8]): Result<(), IoError> {
        let at = path.text();
        match (self.planned(appendFile(at, body))) {
            .Some(error) => .Err(error),
            .None => fsAppendFile(self.handle, at, body),
        }
    }

    fn renameFile(self, source: Path, destination: Path): Result<(), IoError> {
        let start = source.text();
        let end = destination.text();
        match (self.planned(renameFile(start, end))) {
            .Some(error) => .Err(error),
            .None => fsRenameFile(self.handle, start, end),
        }
    }

    fn removeFile(self, path: Path): Result<(), IoError> {
        let at = path.text();
        match (self.planned(removeFile(at))) {
            .Some(error) => .Err(error),
            .None => fsRemoveFile(self.handle, at),
        }
    }

    /// Empty directories only, as a real filesystem's `rmdir(2)` is.
    fn removeDir(self, path: Path): Result<(), IoError> {
        let at = path.text();
        match (self.planned(removeDir(at))) {
            .Some(error) => .Err(error),
            .None => fsRemoveDir(self.handle, at),
        }
    }

    fn makeDir(self, path: Path): Result<(), IoError> {
        let at = path.text();
        match (self.planned(makeDir(at))) {
            .Some(error) => .Err(error),
            .None => fsMakeDir(self.handle, at),
        }
    }

    fn syncFile(self, path: Path): Result<(), IoError> {
        let at = path.text();
        match (self.planned(syncFile(at))) {
            .Some(error) => .Err(error),
            .None => fsSyncFile(self.handle, at),
        }
    }
}

export fn fs(): TestFs {
    TestFs { handle: newFs(), plan: [] }
}

impl TestFs {
    /// A **new** filesystem holding this one's files and these as well, as the
    /// UTF-8 the text spells. The last entry for a path wins, and so does a later
    /// builder: `fs().files(a).files(b)` is `a` overwritten by `b`.
    export fn files(self, entries: [(Str, Str)]): TestFs {
        TestFs { handle: fsFiles(self.handle, entries), plan: self.plan }
    }

    /// The byte twin, for a fixture that is not text. It composes with `files` in
    /// either order, because both write into the one map a file lives in.
    export fn filesBytes(self, entries: [(Str, [U8])]): TestFs {
        TestFs { handle: fsFilesBytes(self.handle, entries), plan: self.plan }
    }

    /// A **new** handle onto the *same* files, through which every write fails
    /// with `.ReadOnly`.
    ///
    /// It holds the inner filesystem rather than a copy of it, so a read through
    /// the attenuated handle sees whatever that filesystem holds *now*. It
    /// forwards `readFile`, `fileExists`, `readDir`, `readFileBytes` and
    /// `syncFile` — `sync` is not a write — and refuses the six that write.
    ///
    /// It attenuates *this* filesystem and no other. A test that wants to
    /// attenuate a filesystem of its own writes an ordinary
    /// `impl<C: FsWrite> FsWrite for Wrapper<C>`, which needs nothing from this
    /// module (SPEC 10.8).
    export fn readOnly(self): TestFs {
        TestFs { handle: fsReadOnly(self.handle), plan: self.plan }
    }

    /// A **new** filesystem onto the same files, failing the calls this plan
    /// names — and a promise that every one of them is made.
    ///
    /// A fault is a call and an error: `readFile(p).fails(e)` fails every matching
    /// call, and `readFile(p).failsOnCall(n, e)` fails the `n`th, counted from one
    /// over the matching calls made through this handle. Matching is the `Eq` the
    /// `FsCall` records derive, so a fault names a call exactly as `calls()`
    /// reports it — same constructor, same arguments.
    ///
    /// **Success comes from the environment and failure comes from the plan.**
    /// `files` says what a read finds; this says what fails. A call the plan fails
    /// is not performed — nothing is written and nothing is removed — and it is
    /// still a call, so `calls()` has it.
    ///
    /// **A fault whose call never happens fails the test.** The runner checks it
    /// at the end of the block, and an unconsumed fault is a failure with the
    /// fault's own spelling in it.
    ///
    /// `faults` **replaces** rather than composing, as `respond` does: two plans
    /// for one call have no answer to which of them wins. The plan it replaced is
    /// retired, and its promise with it.
    export fn faults(self, plan: [FsFault]): TestFs {
        let handle = fsWithPlan(self.handle);
        let _ = describeFsPlan(handle, plan, 0);
        TestFs { handle: handle, plan: plan }
    }

    /// What this filesystem holds at `path`, or `.Err(.NotFound)`.
    ///
    /// The same answer `readFile` gives, reachable without `FsRead`, because
    /// reading an environment back is not performing an effect. A read-back is not
    /// a call, and no fault fires on one.
    export fn read(self, path: Str): Result<Str, IoError> {
        fsRead(self.handle, path)
    }

    /// Every file, as text, **sorted by path**.
    ///
    /// Sorted rather than in the order the writes happened: the order of the
    /// writes is `calls()`'s question and the contents is this one's.
    ///
    /// A directory `makeDir` created holds no octets and is not a file, so it is
    /// not here; `readDir` is the question it answers.
    export fn snapshot(self): [(Str, Str)] {
        fsSnapshot(self.handle)
    }

    /// Every call made through this handle, in the order they completed.
    ///
    /// Through *this* handle: a builder answers a new filesystem and with it a new
    /// log, and so does `readOnly`, so the calls a test reads back are the calls
    /// made through the value it put in the context. Reading the environment back
    /// is not a call — `read`, `snapshot` and `calls` itself are not in it — and a
    /// write that was refused is one, because a refusal is an answer to something
    /// that was asked. A call the plan failed is one too.
    export fn calls(self): [FsCall] {
        fsCalls(self.handle)
    }

    /// The plan's answer for a call about to be made, recorded and consumed.
    ///
    /// The log is what counts the matching calls, so `failsOnCall`'s `nth` is
    /// this call's position among the matching entries `calls()` will report. An
    /// empty plan is answered without reading the log at all.
    fn planned(self, call: FsCall): Option<IoError> {
        if (self.plan.len() == 0) {
            .None
        } else {
            let nth = countingFsCalls(fsCalls(self.handle), call, 0) + 1;
            match (chosenFsFault(self.handle, self.plan, call, nth, 0)) {
                .None => .None,
                .Some(error) => {
                    let _ = noteFsCall(self.handle, call.name, call.path, call.body);
                    .Some(error)
                },
            }
        }
    }
}

/// The first fault in the plan this call matches and this position satisfies,
/// marked as fired.
///
/// The first, so a plan is read in the order it was written. Every entry sees the
/// same `nth` — the count of matching *calls* — so a plan of `failsOnCall(1, a)`
/// and `failsOnCall(2, b)` over one call fails the first with `a` and the second
/// with `b`.
fn chosenFsFault(
    handle: I64,
    plan: [FsFault],
    call: FsCall,
    nth: Int,
    i: Int,
): Option<IoError> {
    match (plan.get(i)) {
        .None => .None,
        .Some(fault) => {
            if (fault.call == call && (fault.nth == 0 || fault.nth == nth)) {
                let _ = noteFault(handle, i);
                .Some(fault.error)
            } else {
                chosenFsFault(handle, plan, call, nth, i + 1)
            }
        },
    }
}

/// How many entries of the log are this call, which is how many matching calls
/// have already been made.
fn countingFsCalls(log: [FsCall], call: FsCall, i: Int): Int {
    match (log.get(i)) {
        .None => 0,
        .Some(seen) => {
            let here = if (seen == call) { 1 } else { 0 };
            here + countingFsCalls(log, call, i + 1)
        },
    }
}

/// Tells the runner what each fault of the plan reads like in a failure message,
/// in the order the plan lists them.
///
/// One call per entry rather than one call with the list, because what crosses is
/// text and two numbers — the whole of what a message needs.
fn describeFsPlan(handle: I64, plan: [FsFault], i: Int): () {
    match (plan.get(i)) {
        .None => (),
        .Some(fault) => {
            let _ = addFsFault(
                handle,
                fault.call.name,
                fault.call.path,
                fault.call.body,
            );
            let _ = faultFails(
                handle,
                fault.nth,
                ioCode(fault.error),
                ioPayload(fault.error),
            );
            describeFsPlan(handle, plan, i + 1)
        },
    }
}

/// An `IoError`'s index in its own declaration order, which `core/effect` states
/// is the ABI — `methodCode`'s rule, for the other enum this module hands over.
fn ioCode(error: IoError): Int {
    match (error) {
        .NotFound => 0,
        .PermissionDenied => 1,
        .ReadOnly => 2,
        .AlreadyExists => 3,
        .NotADirectory => 4,
        .CrossDevice => 5,
        .Other(_) => 6,
    }
}

/// The text `IoError.Other` carries, and the empty string for the six variants
/// that carry nothing.
fn ioPayload(error: IoError): Str {
    match (error) {
        .Other(message) => message,
        _ => "",
    }
}

/// A fresh, empty, writable filesystem, and the handle that names it.
fn newFs(): I64;

/// This view's files with these written over them, in a store of its own, under
/// this view's attenuation and this view's plan.
fn fsFiles(handle: I64, entries: [(Str, Str)]): I64;

/// The byte twin of `fsFiles`.
fn fsFilesBytes(handle: I64, entries: [(Str, [U8])]): I64;

/// A second view onto the same store, refusing every write.
fn fsReadOnly(handle: I64): I64;

/// A view onto the same store with a fresh, empty plan, retiring the plan this
/// one was using — `faults` replaces, so the promise it replaced goes with it.
fn fsWithPlan(handle: I64): I64;

/// The call half of one fault, as a failure message spells it: the `FsCall`'s
/// three fields.
///
/// Two rows for one entry, because a row carrying a call *and* an error would be
/// past the ten integer arguments one call has. `faultFails` is the other half,
/// shared with the network.
fn addFsFault(handle: I64, name: Str, path: Str, body: Str): ();

/// The failure half of the fault just added: the call number `failsOnCall` named
/// or zero for every call, and the error as its variant index and the text it
/// carries.
fn faultFails(handle: I64, nth: Int, code: Int, payload: Str): ();

/// The plan entry at `index` has fired, so its promise is kept.
fn noteFault(handle: I64, index: Int): ();

/// One call, on the path that did not reach the row that would have recorded it.
fn noteFsCall(handle: I64, name: Str, path: Str, body: Str): ();

/// The read-back, without the effect.
fn fsRead(handle: I64, path: Str): Result<Str, IoError>;

/// Every file, as text, sorted by path.
fn fsSnapshot(handle: I64): [(Str, Str)];

/// Every call made through this handle, in the order they completed.
fn fsCalls(handle: I64): [FsCall];

/// The twelve methods of `Fs`, as rows over a handle.
///
/// Free functions rather than methods on `TestFs`, because a `TestFs` is a handle
/// *and* a plan and an argument is flattened into its leaves: a row taking `self`
/// would be handed three values where it expects one.
fn fsReadFile(handle: I64, path: Str): Result<Str, IoError>;

fn fsWriteFile(handle: I64, path: Str, body: Str): Result<(), IoError>;

fn fsFileExists(handle: I64, path: Str): Bool;

fn fsReadDir(handle: I64, path: Str): Result<[Str], IoError>;

fn fsReadFileBytes(handle: I64, path: Str): Result<[U8], IoError>;

fn fsWriteFileBytes(handle: I64, path: Str, body: [U8]): Result<(), IoError>;

fn fsAppendFile(handle: I64, path: Str, body: [U8]): Result<(), IoError>;

fn fsRenameFile(handle: I64, source: Str, destination: Str): Result<(), IoError>;

fn fsRemoveFile(handle: I64, path: Str): Result<(), IoError>;

fn fsRemoveDir(handle: I64, path: Str): Result<(), IoError>;

fn fsMakeDir(handle: I64, path: Str): Result<(), IoError>;

fn fsSyncFile(handle: I64, path: Str): Result<(), IoError>;

// --- Network ------------------------------------------------------------------

/// Refuses every request, until a test says what to answer.
///
/// The default is `.Err(.Refused)` and not a stub `200`: a test that reaches the
/// network by accident should say so at its assertion rather than pass on an
/// answer nobody wrote.
///
/// ## The one double whose configuration is not a handle
///
/// A responder is not state, it is **behaviour**, and the runner cannot hold it.
/// Invoking a function value would mean calling back into monomorphized Buri with
/// a `Request` the archive had rebuilt, and the answer would have to cross as a
/// `Result<Response, NetError>`, which `cli/runtime/lib.rs` §2.1 refuses: it
/// restricts an error variant to carrying no fields, and `NetError` carries a
/// `Str` on `BadUrl` and on `Transport`.
///
/// So `fetch` is a Buri body that calls the responder directly, and neither
/// runtime table names it. SPEC 10.6 is what makes that safe: a lambda may not
/// capture an effect-carrying value, so a
/// `fn(Request) => Result<Response, NetError>` is a pure function of the request.
///
/// The consequence for whoever writes one: a responder cannot take `ctx`, so it
/// cannot call `http.text(ctx, ...)` inside itself. It can build a `Response`
/// literal, and it can **capture** a `Response` the test built before it. Both
/// spellings are in `docs/reference/build/testing.md`.
///
/// The handle beside the responder names the **log**, which `calls()` reads. The
/// third field is the **fault plan**, in the value because a `NetError` carries a
/// `Str` on two variants and could not come back out of a row.
export struct TestNet {
    handle: I64,
    responder: fn(Request) => Result<Response, NetError>,
    plan: [NetFault],
}

impl Net for TestNet {
    /// The plan first, the responder second, and the log last.
    ///
    /// A request the plan fails never reaches the responder, so `respond` and
    /// `faults` answer two different questions. The log is last because a call is
    /// recorded on the way out, and a failed request is a request, so both paths
    /// record.
    fn fetch(self, request: Request): Result<Response, NetError> {
        let answer: Result<Response, NetError> = match (self.planned(fetch(request))) {
            .Some(error) => .Err(error),
            .None => {
                let respond = self.responder;
                respond(request)
            },
        };
        let _ = recordFetch(
            self.handle,
            methodCode(request.method),
            request.url,
            request.headers,
            request.body,
        );
        answer
    }
}

export fn net(): TestNet {
    TestNet { handle: newNet(), responder: fn(_request) => .Err(.Refused), plan: [] }
}

/// A fresh, empty log, and the handle that names it.
fn newNet(): I64;

/// A fresh, empty log carrying this one's plan, for the builder that answers a
/// new network without changing what it promised.
fn netRebind(handle: I64): I64;

/// A fresh, empty log with a fresh, empty plan, retiring the plan this handle was
/// using.
fn netWithPlan(handle: I64): I64;

/// The call half of one fault of a network's plan. The URL and not the whole
/// request, because a message that named every header would be a paragraph;
/// matching still reads every field, through `NetCall`'s `Eq`. `faultFails` is
/// the other half, shared with the filesystem.
fn addNetFault(handle: I64, url: Str): ();

/// One request, recorded after the responder has answered it.
///
/// The request arrives in pieces: a `Method` is its variant index by the time the
/// runner sees it (`methodCode`), and the runner puts the four values back
/// together on the way out. They are the four `core/host`'s real `fetch` hands
/// over, in the same order.
fn recordFetch(handle: I64, method: Int, url: Str, headers: [Header], body: [U8]): ();

/// A `Method`'s index in its own declaration order, which `core/effect` states is
/// the ABI. This is the one place in this module that spells it out.
fn methodCode(method: Method): Int {
    match (method) {
        .Get => 0,
        .Head => 1,
        .Post => 2,
        .Put => 3,
        .Patch => 4,
        .Delete => 5,
        .Options => 6,
    }
}

/// A `NetError`'s index in its own declaration order — `ioCode`'s rule, for the
/// error this double fails with.
fn netCode(error: NetError): Int {
    match (error) {
        .Timeout => 0,
        .Refused => 1,
        .BadUrl(_) => 2,
        .Transport(_) => 3,
        .Aborted => 4,
    }
}

/// The text `NetError`'s two payload-carrying variants carry, and the empty
/// string for the three that carry nothing.
fn netPayload(error: NetError): Str {
    match (error) {
        .BadUrl(message) => message,
        .Transport(message) => message,
        _ => "",
    }
}

impl TestNet {
    /// A **new** network answering through this function, which is handed every
    /// request the code under test makes and either answers it or fails it.
    ///
    /// One responder, not a routing table: a responder that answers two URLs
    /// differently matches on `request.path()`, on `request.method`, or on
    /// `request.header(...)`. `respond` **replaces** rather than composing, since
    /// two responders for one request have no answer to which of them wins.
    export fn respond(
        self,
        responder: fn(Request) => Result<Response, NetError>,
    ): TestNet {
        TestNet {
            handle: netRebind(self.handle),
            responder: responder,
            plan: self.plan,
        }
    }

    /// A **new** network failing the requests this plan names — and a promise that
    /// every one of them is made.
    ///
    /// `TestFs::faults` states the whole rule, and it is the same rule here.
    /// Matching is `NetCall`'s derived `Eq`, so two requests differing in a header
    /// are two calls and a fault names one of them. `fails` fails every matching
    /// request and `failsOnCall(n, e)` the `n`th. A failed request never reaches
    /// the responder and is still in `calls()`. A fault whose request is never
    /// made fails the test.
    export fn faults(self, plan: [NetFault]): TestNet {
        let handle = netWithPlan(self.handle);
        let _ = describeNetPlan(handle, plan, 0);
        TestNet { handle: handle, responder: self.responder, plan: plan }
    }

    /// Every request made through this handle, in the order they were answered.
    ///
    /// `respond` mints a fresh log along with the network it answers: a network
    /// that shared its receiver's log would report calls made to a different
    /// network.
    export fn calls(self): [NetCall] {
        netCalls(self.handle)
    }

    /// The plan's answer for a request about to be made, recorded and consumed.
    ///
    /// `TestFs::planned`, for the other double: the log counts the matching
    /// requests, and an empty plan is answered without reading it.
    fn planned(self, call: NetCall): Option<NetError> {
        if (self.plan.len() == 0) {
            .None
        } else {
            let nth = countingNetCalls(netCalls(self.handle), call, 0) + 1;
            chosenNetFault(self.handle, self.plan, call, nth, 0)
        }
    }
}

/// `chosenFsFault`, for the network. One rule written at two types, because a
/// `calls()` answering one list cannot hold two error types.
fn chosenNetFault(
    handle: I64,
    plan: [NetFault],
    call: NetCall,
    nth: Int,
    i: Int,
): Option<NetError> {
    match (plan.get(i)) {
        .None => .None,
        .Some(fault) => {
            if (fault.call == call && (fault.nth == 0 || fault.nth == nth)) {
                let _ = noteFault(handle, i);
                .Some(fault.error)
            } else {
                chosenNetFault(handle, plan, call, nth, i + 1)
            }
        },
    }
}

/// How many entries of the log are this request.
fn countingNetCalls(log: [NetCall], call: NetCall, i: Int): Int {
    match (log.get(i)) {
        .None => 0,
        .Some(seen) => {
            let here = if (seen == call) { 1 } else { 0 };
            here + countingNetCalls(log, call, i + 1)
        },
    }
}

/// `describeFsPlan`, for the network.
fn describeNetPlan(handle: I64, plan: [NetFault], i: Int): () {
    match (plan.get(i)) {
        .None => (),
        .Some(fault) => {
            let request = fault.call.0;
            let _ = addNetFault(handle, request.url);
            let _ = faultFails(
                handle,
                fault.nth,
                netCode(fault.error),
                netPayload(fault.error),
            );
            describeNetPlan(handle, plan, i + 1)
        },
    }
}

/// The log itself, read by the handle rather than by the value that names it.
///
/// An argument is flattened into its leaves, so a method taking `self` would hand
/// the runner the responder closure as well. Every other `calls()` is a row; this
/// one is a Buri body over a row that takes the handle.
fn netCalls(handle: I64): [NetCall];

// --- Clock --------------------------------------------------------------------

/// Starts at zero and advances only when the test advances it. `sleepMillis`
/// advances it without sleeping, which is the whole point of a test clock.
export struct TestClock(I64);

impl Clock for TestClock {
    fn nowMillis(self): I64;

    fn sleepMillis(self, millis: Int): ();
}

export fn clock(): TestClock;

impl TestClock {
    /// A **new** clock, at that instant. The one it was called on is untouched,
    /// so a test holding both holds two independent clocks.
    export fn at(self, millis: Int): TestClock;
}

// --- Randomness ---------------------------------------------------------------

/// Seeded — at zero unless a test says otherwise — so a failure reproduces.
export struct TestRand(I64);

impl Rand for TestRand {
    fn nextInt(self, lo: Int, hi: Int): Int;

    fn nextFloat(self): Float;
}

export fn rand(): TestRand;

impl TestRand {
    /// A **new** generator, at that seed and drawing from the start of its
    /// sequence.
    export fn seed(self, n: Int): TestRand;
}

// --- Entropy ------------------------------------------------------------------

/// Seeded, like `rand()`, and that is the **only** thing in this language allowed
/// to answer `Entropy` predictably.
///
/// `core/effect`'s `Entropy` promises octets nobody can guess, and a test needs
/// the opposite: the same octets every run. A test source is the only place
/// `entropy()` can be named, and `core/host`'s `entropy` is the only thing a
/// program can name.
///
/// The sequence is `rand()`'s, byte for byte and seed for seed, and it is the
/// same sequence on both backends.
export struct TestEntropy(I64);

impl Entropy for TestEntropy {
    fn bytes(self, count: Int): [U8];
}

export fn entropy(): TestEntropy;

impl TestEntropy {
    /// A **new** generator, at that seed and drawing from the start of its
    /// sequence.
    export fn seed(self, n: Int): TestEntropy;
}

// --- Environment --------------------------------------------------------------

/// Empty: no variables and no arguments, whatever the host happens to have.
export struct TestEnv(I64);

impl Env for TestEnv {
    fn variable(self, name: Str): Option<Str>;

    fn args(self): [Str];
}

export fn env(): TestEnv;

impl TestEnv {
    /// A **new** environment with these variables and this one's arguments, so
    /// the two builders compose in either order. The last binding of a name wins.
    export fn variables(self, vars: [(Str, Str)]): TestEnv;

    /// A **new** environment with these arguments and this one's variables.
    export fn arguments(self, args: [Str]): TestEnv;
}

// --- Process ------------------------------------------------------------------

/// Absorbs the exit rather than taking it. A test that ended the process would
/// take every block after it with it, and the runner would report a suite that
/// stopped rather than a function that exited.
///
/// `proc()` has no readers. What a test asserts about a function that exits is
/// that the *test* carried on, which the assertions after the call say. The exit
/// code is not kept, so this double has no runner-side state and its handle names
/// nothing.
export struct TestProc(I64);

impl Proc for TestProc {
    fn exitWith(self, code: Int): () {
        let _ = code;
    }
}

export fn proc(): TestProc {
    TestProc(0)
}

// --- Sockets ------------------------------------------------------------------

/// Sockets with no network behind them: `open` mints one, `sent` reads back what
/// was pushed on it.
///
/// A program that holds a `Socket` and pushes on it — an actor a hook handed one
/// to, a room that broadcasts to everybody in it — is bounded by `Sockets` and by
/// nothing else, so it can be tested with no listener, no port, no client and no
/// server. `sockets().open()` is a socket, a `send` on it is recorded, and
/// `sent()` is the whole of what the far side would have received.
///
/// **`Listen` deliberately has no double.** Reading from a socket is `Listen`'s
/// authority and writing on one is `Sockets`'. A fake acceptor is a value a test
/// writes for itself; `lib/semantics`' `Scripted` is one. This half cannot be
/// written by hand, because an effect method takes only `self` and `self` is
/// immutable, so a hand-written `Sockets` records nothing.
///
/// **Nothing here waits.** The real `socketSendText` never waits either, so a
/// double that records the push and returns keeps the same promise. There is no
/// deadline, no timeout and no thread in it.
export struct TestSockets(I64);

impl Sockets for TestSockets {
    /// Recorded, and read back by `sent` as a `.Text`.
    ///
    /// **Dropped where the socket is not an open one of this double's**: a handle
    /// a program invented, a socket this double closed, and a socket another
    /// `sockets()` minted are all treated alike. Two doubles in one test are two
    /// worlds, exactly as two `fs()` calls are two filesystems.
    fn socketSendText(self, socket: Int, text: Str): ();

    /// The same, read back as a `.Binary`.
    fn socketSendBytes(self, socket: Int, body: [U8]): ();

    /// Closes the socket, so that `isOpen` answers `false` and every later send
    /// through it is dropped.
    ///
    /// **The code and the phrase are not kept**: they are what the far side is
    /// told, and there is no far side. What a close does here is close, which
    /// `isOpen` reports directly and `sent` reports by not growing.
    fn socketClose(self, socket: Int, code: Int, reason: Str): ();
}

/// No sockets open and nothing pushed. A fresh double every call, so what one
/// test opens is invisible to the next.
export fn sockets(): TestSockets;

impl TestSockets {
    /// A socket of this double's: open, and with no network behind it.
    ///
    /// It is a `Socket` like any other: inert, comparable, copyable and sendable
    /// to an actor. The number inside it is this runner's, so two sockets are
    /// never equal and a socket is never equal to one another `sockets()` minted.
    ///
    /// A **method** rather than a builder: it answers a new socket of the double
    /// it was called on, and the double itself does not move.
    export fn open(self): Socket {
        Socket(socketsOpen(self.0))
    }

    /// Every message pushed on a socket of this double's, oldest first.
    ///
    /// Both framings in one list and in one order, because that is the order the
    /// far side would have seen them in. A broadcast that reached two sockets is
    /// two entries here.
    ///
    /// The pair is built **in Buri**, out of a flat struct the runner hands over,
    /// which is how a non-enum value crosses whole (`cli/runtime/lib.rs` §2.1).
    /// The context `map` asks for is this module's own `alloc()`, which charges
    /// nothing, so this stays a reader taking `self` and no context.
    export fn sent(self): [(Socket, Message)] {
        socketsSent(self.0).map(alloc(), fn(one) => (Socket(one.socket), framed(one)))
    }

    /// Whether that socket is still one this double will accept a message for.
    ///
    /// `false` for a socket this double closed, and `false` for a handle it never
    /// minted.
    ///
    /// **A question about this socket, and never a count of the open ones.** A
    /// count would be a claim about the whole runner, and the blocks of a suite
    /// share a process.
    export fn isOpen(self, socket: Socket): Bool {
        socketsIsOpen(self.0, socket.0)
    }

    /// A WebSocket client that dials this double: a socket per dial, and
    /// `messages` delivered on it in order.
    ///
    /// **Every dial delivers the script again**, so a reconnect loop — the shape
    /// `core/net/websocket` documents, a loop around `connect` with a sleep in it
    /// — gets a second session rather than a socket that was already spent.
    ///
    /// Two `dialling` calls are two clients on one double, and two `sockets()`
    /// calls are two worlds. See `TestWebSocketClient` for what the client does
    /// with the script.
    export fn dialling(self, messages: [Message]): TestWebSocketClient {
        TestWebSocketClient(socketsDialling(self.0, messages))
    }
}

/// One push, as the runner hands it back: which socket, which framing, and the
/// two payload fields of which a framing names one.
///
/// Private, because `sent` is the surface and this is only the crossing. `Frame`
/// is `core/effect`'s, and its `.Closed` is unreachable here, because only the two
/// sends ever write this log.
struct Sent {
    socket: Int,
    frame: Frame,
    text: Str,
    data: [U8],
}

/// One recorded push, as the message a reader wants.
fn framed(one: Sent): Message {
    match (one.frame) {
        .Text => .Text(one.text),
        .Binary => .Binary(one.data),
        // Unreachable: nothing but the two sends writes this log, and neither
        // says `.Closed`. A total answer costs nothing, and an abort here would
        // turn a toolchain bug into a stopped test.
        .Closed => .Text(one.text),
    }
}

/// A fresh socket of this double's, and the number that names it.
fn socketsOpen(handle: I64): Int;

/// Every push through this double, oldest first.
fn socketsSent(handle: I64): [Sent];

/// Whether this double minted that socket and has not closed it.
fn socketsIsOpen(handle: I64, socket: Int): Bool;

// --- WebSocketClient ----------------------------------------------------------

/// A WebSocket client with a script instead of a network: every time it dials it
/// delivers the messages it was given in order, and then closes normally.
///
/// **It is minted by a `sockets()` rather than minting sockets of its own.** The
/// socket `connect` gets is one of that double's, so everything a program pushes
/// is recorded by its `sent()` and a program that closes shows up in its
/// `isOpen`. This type implements `WebSocketClient` and nothing else.
///
/// It is a method on `TestSockets` rather than a builder of its own because a
/// `TestSockets` carries an effect, so it may be passed only as `ctx` or as
/// `self` (`effect-param-not-ctx`).
///
/// ```buri ignore why="a test source is the one place a `test` block is legal, and this file is not one"
/// from "core/effect" import { Sockets, WebSocketClient };
/// from "core/host/testing" import * as testing;
/// from "core/net/websocket" import * as websocket;
/// from "core/testing/assert" import * as assert;
///
/// test "a client counts what it was pushed" {
///     let pushes = testing.sockets();
///     let ctx = context {
///         Sockets: pushes,
///         WebSocketClient: pushes.dialling([.Text("one"), .Text("two")]),
///     };
///     let closed = websocket.connect(ctx, websocket.Client {
///         url: "ws://example/socket",
///         onOpen: fn(_c, _socket, _response) => 0,
///         onMessage: fn(_c, _socket, seen, _message) => seen + 1,
///         onClose: fn(_c, _socket, _seen, _reason) => (),
///     });
///     assert.eq(closed, .Ok(.Normal));
/// }
/// ```
///
/// **Nothing here waits.** There is no timer, no thread and no deadline
/// anywhere in it: the script is a list that is already in memory, and
/// `connectReceive` takes the next thing off it.
export struct TestWebSocketClient(I64);

impl WebSocketClient for TestWebSocketClient {
    /// Mints a socket on the `sockets()` double this one was built with, and
    /// answers it with a `101` carrying no headers.
    ///
    /// **A second dial is a second session**, with the script back at its first
    /// message, so a reconnect loop reads the same frames it read the first time.
    ///
    /// **A URL that is not `ws://` or `wss://` is `.Err(.Unsupported)`**, the
    /// cause a real client gives a scheme it cannot speak. A test that wants to
    /// check what its program does when the socket never opens writes a URL that
    /// cannot be dialled.
    fn connectSocket(self, url: Str): Result<Connected, ServeError>;

    /// The next scripted message, as `.Text` or `.Binary`.
    ///
    /// The script running out is a close with code 1000, which
    /// `core/net/websocket` reads as `.Normal`. So is a socket the program has
    /// closed, because a closed socket delivers nothing more. After that close
    /// the socket is gone and a further call is `.Err(.Closed)` — the same last
    /// answer `listenReceive` gives, so a loop written against one works against
    /// the other.
    fn connectReceive(self, socket: Int): Result<Received, ServeError>;
}

/// The client's socket, minted on the double that will record what is pushed on
/// it, and the number that names the client.
fn socketsDialling(handle: I64, messages: [Message]): I64;

derive Eq, Show for FsCall;
// --- The call log -------------------------------------------------------------
//
// One record per double, and one constructor per method. A `Call` is what was
// asked and never what came back: the answer is the return value the test
// already has.

/// One call to a `TestFs`, as `calls()` reports it and as a test writes it down.
///
/// A record with private fields, so the constructors below are the only way to
/// build one. `derive Eq` is what an assertion compares — two calls differ as
/// soon as an argument does — and `derive Show` is what a failing one prints.
///
/// One record rather than an enum of eleven variants. Every method of `Fs` takes
/// a path and at most one thing more, so `path` is the first argument and `body`
/// the second — the text written, `renameFile`'s destination, or the empty string
/// where the method takes only a path. Octets are recorded as **the text they
/// spell**, as `snapshot()` records them.
export struct FsCall {
    name: Str,
    path: Str,
    body: Str,
}

/// A `readFile(path)`, as a call.
export fn readFile(path: Str): FsCall {
    FsCall { name: "readFile", path: path, body: "" }
}

/// A `writeFile(path, body)`, as a call.
export fn writeFile(path: Str, body: Str): FsCall {
    FsCall { name: "writeFile", path: path, body: body }
}

/// A `fileExists(path)`, as a call. It cannot fail, and it is still something the
/// code under test asked the filesystem.
export fn fileExists(path: Str): FsCall {
    FsCall { name: "fileExists", path: path, body: "" }
}

/// A `readDir(path)`, as a call.
export fn readDir(path: Str): FsCall {
    FsCall { name: "readDir", path: path, body: "" }
}

/// A `readFileBytes(path)`, as a call. The same path `readFile` would name, and a
/// different call.
export fn readFileBytes(path: Str): FsCall {
    FsCall { name: "readFileBytes", path: path, body: "" }
}

/// A `writeFileBytes(path, body)`, as a call.
export fn writeFileBytes(path: Str, body: [U8]): FsCall {
    FsCall { name: "writeFileBytes", path: path, body: spelled(body) }
}

/// An `appendFile(path, body)`, as a call.
export fn appendFile(path: Str, body: [U8]): FsCall {
    FsCall { name: "appendFile", path: path, body: spelled(body) }
}

/// A `renameFile(source, destination)`, as a call. The source is the path and the
/// destination is the second argument, as it is in the call itself.
export fn renameFile(source: Str, destination: Str): FsCall {
    FsCall { name: "renameFile", path: source, body: destination }
}

/// A `removeFile(path)`, as a call.
export fn removeFile(path: Str): FsCall {
    FsCall { name: "removeFile", path: path, body: "" }
}

/// A `removeDir(path)`, as a call.
export fn removeDir(path: Str): FsCall {
    FsCall { name: "removeDir", path: path, body: "" }
}

/// A `makeDir(path)`, as a call.
export fn makeDir(path: Str): FsCall {
    FsCall { name: "makeDir", path: path, body: "" }
}

/// A `syncFile(path)`, as a call.
export fn syncFile(path: Str): FsCall {
    FsCall { name: "syncFile", path: path, body: "" }
}

/// The text these octets spell, exactly as `readFile` reads back what
/// `writeFileBytes` wrote.
///
/// Lossy on purpose: a log records what a program asked to write, and a fixture
/// that is not text is still a call that was made. It is the runner's decode
/// rather than `core/bytes`', because a constructor takes no context.
fn spelled(body: [U8]): Str;

derive Eq, Show for NetCall;
/// One request made through a `TestNet`.
///
/// The whole request, because `Net` has one method and it takes one value: two
/// requests that differ in a header are two calls.
export struct NetCall(Request);

/// A `fetch(request)`, as a call.
export fn fetch(request: Request): NetCall {
    NetCall(request)
}

derive Eq, Show for StdinCall;
/// One read made through a `TestStdin`.
///
/// `count` is the number of octets `readBytes` asked for, and zero for
/// `readLine`, which asks for a line and names no number.
export struct StdinCall {
    name: Str,
    count: Int,
}

/// A `readLine()`, as a call.
export fn readLine(): StdinCall {
    StdinCall { name: "readLine", count: 0 }
}

/// A `readBytes(n)`, as a call.
export fn readBytes(n: Int): StdinCall {
    StdinCall { name: "readBytes", count: n }
}

// --- The fault plan -----------------------------------------------------------
//
// A plan is a list of faults and a fault is a call and an error. Success comes
// from the environment — `files`, `respond` — and failure comes from here.
//
// One `Fault` record per double, as there is one `Call` record per double. A
// double's error type is fixed by the double, so `FsCall::fails` takes an
// `IoError` and `NetCall::fails` takes a `NetError`.
//
// Neither `Fault` record derives anything: a fault is matched by its call, and
// the runner prints it from the pieces `addFsFault` sends.

/// One planned failure of a `TestFs`.
///
/// `nth` is the call number `failsOnCall` named, counted from one over the
/// matching calls, and **zero for `fails`**, which is every call. Zero rather than
/// an `Option<Int>` because there is no zeroth call, so the record stays flat.
export struct FsFault {
    call: FsCall,
    error: IoError,
    nth: Int,
}

impl FsCall {
    /// This call fails with this error, every time it is made.
    export fn fails(self, error: IoError): FsFault {
        FsFault { call: self, error: error, nth: 0 }
    }

    /// The `nth` matching call fails with this error, counted from one, and every
    /// other one is answered by the filesystem.
    ///
    /// The *matching* call: a plan that fails the second `appendFile("wal", b)`
    /// counts appends of `b` to `wal` and not calls in general.
    export fn failsOnCall(self, nth: Int, error: IoError): FsFault {
        FsFault { call: self, error: error, nth: nth }
    }
}

/// One planned failure of a `TestNet`. `FsFault`'s two fields at the network's
/// two types.
export struct NetFault {
    call: NetCall,
    error: NetError,
    nth: Int,
}

impl NetCall {
    /// This request fails with this error, every time it is made — and it never
    /// reaches the responder.
    export fn fails(self, error: NetError): NetFault {
        NetFault { call: self, error: error, nth: 0 }
    }

    /// The `nth` matching request fails, counted from one. The retry a test is
    /// about to assert on is `failsOnCall(1, .Timeout)` and a responder.
    export fn failsOnCall(self, nth: Int, error: NetError): NetFault {
        NetFault { call: self, error: error, nth: nth }
    }
}

// --- Tasks --------------------------------------------------------------------

/// Runs every task, one at a time, in an order the test chooses — and by default
/// in the order the items are written.
///
/// `Tasks.parallel` promises its *results* in the items' order and promises
/// nothing about the order the work happens in. This double makes that order a
/// value: `tasks()` runs the tasks in program order, `anyOrder()` runs them in one
/// seeded order, and `everyOrder()` runs the whole `test` body once per completion
/// order. Nothing here is concurrent, so a task runs to completion before the next
/// one starts and `calls()` reports them in that order.
///
/// **A task `core/tasks`'s `spawn` queued is one of them.** A scope runs a round
/// of what was spawned through `Tasks.parallel`, so this double decides which
/// spawned task goes first.
///
/// ## The seed is the order's own number
///
/// There are `n!` orders of `n` tasks, and a seed is which of them, counted from
/// zero in the order the orders themselves sort in: `seed(0)` is program order,
/// `seed(1)` swaps the last two, and the last seed is the reverse. So a seed
/// **replays**: `everyOrder`'s fourth run and `seed(3)` are the same order. A seed
/// past the last one wraps, so any number is a legal seed at every length.
///
/// `anyOrder()` with no seed is the order **this suite's own content** names: the
/// runner derives it from the suite's action key, the hash the result cache is
/// already keyed on. So the order a suite schedules in changes exactly when the
/// verdict that order produced stops being reusable, and never on a run that
/// changed nothing. A block that means one particular order says `seed(n)`, and
/// the failure report names the seed that replays the one it hit.
///
/// A task has no error to carry — `parallel` answers `[B]` — so a fault here is an
/// *index*, a count and a sentence, all three of which cross the boundary. This
/// double is a bare handle where `TestFs` and `TestNet` carry a plan as well.
export struct TestTasks(I64);

impl Tasks for TestTasks {
    /// Every task once, in the order this double schedules them, and the results
    /// in the items' order.
    ///
    /// The runtime drives the walk, through the same closure trampoline
    /// `host.HostTasks.parallel` uses, so a test through this double crosses the
    /// same boundary the real implementation crosses.
    ///
    /// `ctx` is the caller's whole context and is what every step is handed —
    /// `Tasks`' own promise, and the same parameter the real implementation
    /// takes.
    fn parallel<C, A, B>(self, ctx: C, items: [A], f: fn(C, Int, A) => B): [B];
}

/// Program order, no faults, and a fresh log.
export fn tasks(): TestTasks;

impl TestTasks {
    /// A **new** scheduler running its tasks in one seeded order.
    ///
    /// The default seed is the reverse of program order; `seed(n)` names another.
    /// One order per run of the body, not one per `parallel`: two fan-outs of the
    /// same length in one block are scheduled the same way.
    export fn anyOrder(self): TestTasks;

    /// A **new** scheduler that runs the whole `test` body once per completion
    /// order — `n!` times for a `parallel` over `n` items.
    ///
    /// The body, not the fan-out: every run builds its own doubles from the same
    /// lines, so the runs are independent and the assertion at the end of the body
    /// is an assertion about *every* order.
    ///
    /// Six items are 720 runs of the body and seven are 5040, so this **refuses
    /// above six** and says so. A fan-out that wide is `anyOrder`'s question.
    ///
    /// The first failing order ends the block, because a failed assertion is an
    /// abort and there is nothing to catch (SPEC 6.9). The report names the order
    /// that failed and the seed that replays it, and the orders after it do not
    /// run.
    export fn everyOrder(self): TestTasks;

    /// A **new** scheduler at that seed — the order's own number, counted from
    /// zero, wrapping past the last.
    ///
    /// `anyOrder` with the choice named, and what a failure report prints for a
    /// reader to paste back: `seed(3)` is `everyOrder`'s fourth run, exactly.
    export fn seed(self, n: Int): TestTasks;

    /// A **new** scheduler failing the tasks this plan names — and a promise that
    /// every one of them is reached.
    ///
    /// A task has no error channel to answer through, so a task the plan fails is
    /// a task that ends the program. A fault is therefore a call and a **reason**,
    /// and the block fails with that reason. The tasks scheduled before it have run
    /// and had their effects; the ones after it have not started.
    ///
    /// The rules are `TestFs::faults`' rules. `task(k).fails(why)` fails that task
    /// every time it is reached, and `task(k).failsOnCall(n, why)` the `n`th time,
    /// counted from one over the matching tasks — which counts `parallel` calls,
    /// since one call reaches each index once. A fault whose task is never reached
    /// fails the test. `faults` replaces rather than composing, and retires the
    /// plan it replaced.
    export fn faults(self, plan: [TaskFault]): TestTasks {
        let planned = self.replan();
        let _ = describeTaskPlan(planned, plan, 0);
        planned
    }

    /// Every task this scheduler ran, in the order they completed.
    ///
    /// The order the *work* happened in, which is the only place that order is
    /// observable: the result list is in the items' order whatever this says. A
    /// task the plan failed is not here, because it never completed.
    export fn calls(self): [TaskCall];

    /// Which run of the `test` body this is, counted from one.
    ///
    /// One for a block that is not being re-run. Under `everyOrder` it is the
    /// number of the order this run is using, plus one — `seed(runs() - 1)` is this
    /// run's order.
    ///
    /// The answer is the **block**'s rather than this handle's, so a block with two
    /// schedulers in it is still one block being run again.
    export fn runs(self): Int;

    /// How many runs of the body there will be: `n!` under `everyOrder` once a
    /// `parallel` has said what `n` is, and one otherwise.
    ///
    /// One before the first `parallel`, because until then nothing knows how many
    /// tasks there are to order.
    export fn orders(self): Int;

    /// A **new** scheduler with a fresh, empty plan and this one's ordering,
    /// retiring the plan it was using.
    fn replan(self): TestTasks;

    /// One entry of a plan: which task, on which call, and why — the three pieces
    /// a failure message is assembled from, and the three the runner matches on.
    fn addFault(self, index: Int, nth: Int, reason: Str): ();
}

/// Hands the runner each entry of a plan as it is installed.
///
/// `describeFsPlan`'s walk, one row per entry.
fn describeTaskPlan(ctx: TestTasks, plan: [TaskFault], i: Int): () {
    match (plan.get(i)) {
        .None => (),
        .Some(fault) => {
            let _ = ctx.addFault(fault.call.index, fault.nth, fault.reason);
            describeTaskPlan(ctx, plan, i + 1)
        },
    }
}

derive Eq, Show for TaskCall;
/// One task of one `parallel`, named by its index — as `calls()` reports it and
/// as a plan names it.
///
/// The index and not the item: a task is identified by where it is in the fan-out,
/// which is the only thing the effect's signature promises about it. An item would
/// have to be `Show`n to be recorded and `Eq` to be matched, and `parallel` bounds
/// neither.
export struct TaskCall {
    index: Int,
}

/// The task at that index, as a call.
export fn task(index: Int): TaskCall {
    TaskCall { index: index }
}

/// One planned failure of a `TestTasks`. `FsFault`'s three fields, with a reason
/// where the filesystem has an error: a task answers `B` and has no error channel,
/// so what a fault carries is what the failure will say.
export struct TaskFault {
    call: TaskCall,
    reason: Str,
    nth: Int,
}

impl TaskCall {
    /// This task fails with this reason, every time it is reached.
    export fn fails(self, reason: Str): TaskFault {
        TaskFault { call: self, reason: reason, nth: 0 }
    }

    /// The `nth` time this task is reached it fails, counted from one over the
    /// matching tasks — which is once per `parallel`, so `n` is the number of the
    /// fan-out rather than of the task.
    export fn failsOnCall(self, nth: Int, reason: Str): TaskFault {
        TaskFault { call: self, reason: reason, nth: nth }
    }
}
